 ],
 "literal": "It hurts, my heart, it hurts, my heart! / Bearing tears that are like burning the eyelids, / our lover who was suited to be patient / becoming one who lacks patience, it hurts, my heart!",
 "translation": "It hurts, my heart, it hurts, my heart! Bearing tears that burn like fire on my eyelids, our lover, who once seemed so capable of patience and comforting me, has now become completely impatient. This hurts, my heart!",
 "modern": "என் நெஞ்சமே நோகிறது, என் நெஞ்சமே நோகிறது! இமைகளைச் சுட்டெரிப்பது போன்ற கண்ணீரைத் தாங்கிக்கொண்டு, என்னைத் தேற்றுவதற்கும் அமைதிப்படுத்துவதற்கும் தகுதியுடையவராக இருந்த நம் காதலர், இன்று அமைதியற்றவராக மாறிவிட்டாரே! அது என் நெஞ்சை மிகவும் நோகச் செய்கிறது.",
 "explanation": "In this Neytal (seashore) poem, the heroine expresses her deep anguish to her confidante or to her own heart during the painful period of separation. She describes her tears as being so hot that they feel like they are burning her eyelids as she tries to hold them back. She laments that her lover, who was once perfectly suited to be patient, comforting, and reassuring, has now become completely impatient and indifferent to her suffering. This sudden change in his nature hurts her deeply, intensifying her grief. The imagery of burning tears perfectly captures the agonizing wait and sorrow associated with the coastal landscape.",
